All code and data are available in the Supplementary Material on [OSF.io](https://osf.io/uymf7/overview?view_only=e857249268d9465996d04e28d39c9e14).

## Population network construction

We generated one population network for each $p \in$ {6, 12, 18} nodes with a fixed density of 0.25 (25% of all possible edges present) to serve as data-generating structures.
Edge weights were sampled from the uniform distribution with values between 0.10-0.25, then randomly placed in a $p$-dimensional matrix.
These characteristics were chosen such that resulting structures resemble real psychological networks [e.g., @wysocki2021], while ensuring positive definiteness of $\symbf{\Sigma}_\text{emp}$ in all conditions.
Dissimilarity was introduced in two ways: *Rewiring* [@watts1998] -- reconnecting one endpoint of an existing edge to another randomly chosen node -- was applied such that each edge was rewired with probability $p(r) \in$ {0, 0.25, 0.50}, while keeping density constant.
*Density* was varied by removing or adding randomly sampled edges from/to the base network.
This percentage was $p(d) = \pm 25\%$ and $\pm 50\%$ removed or added edges for $p = 6$, and was proportionally lowered for $p = 12$ (to $\pm 19\%$ and $\pm 38\%$) and $p = 18$ (to $\pm 13\%$ and $\pm 26\%$ edges) to ensure positive definiteness of $\symbf{\Sigma}_\text{emp}$.
Additional information on network structures is provided in the Supplementary Material.

