## Simulation Design and Procedure

Based on the population networks, in each condition, we simulated a standardized multivariate normal dataset ($\mathbf{X} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0}, \symbf{\Sigma}_\text{emp})$, where $\symbf{\Sigma}_\text{emp}$ is the correlation matrix corresponding to $\symbf\Omega_\text{emp}$), with $N \in$ {100, 250, 500, 1000, 2500, 5000} observations, using the `mvrnorm` function from the `MASS` package [@ripley2025].
Depending on the condition, one of the generated base networks was chosen as a data-generating structure: 
In conditions where the $H_0$ of network equality was true (correct specification), the theoretical network $\symbf\Omega_\text{theo}$ matched the $\symbf\Omega_\text{emp}$ used to generate the empirical data.
When $H_0$ was false (misspecification), the data-generating $\symbf\Omega_\text{emp}$ differed from $\symbf\Omega_\text{theo}$ with respect to structure or density.
A GGM $\symbf{\hat\Omega}_\text{emp}$ was obtained from each dataset by either calculating the raw partial correlation matrix or using glasso regularization with EBIC, ggmModSelect stepwise model search [both using the `qgraph` package, version `r packageVersion("qgraph")`\; @epskamp2012], nonconvex atan regularization [@wang2016], or nodewise regression with BIC stepwise search [both using the `mantar` package, version `r packageVersion("mantar")`\; @nehler2026].
Confirmatory test statistics and indices were then computed.
Mantel's and Fisher's tests were computed using $\symbf{\hat{\Omega}}_\text{emp}$ as input, while all other methods used $\symbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_\text{emp}$, except the Cai-Ma test, which requires raw data. 

Bootstrap-calibrated $p$-values were obtained via a parametric bootstrap under $H_0$ by simulating 1000 bootstrap samples with `mvrnorm` from $\symbf\Sigma_\text{theo}$ in each replication, rerunning the full network-estimation pipeline, and comparing the observed test statistics against their bootstrapped null distribution.
This resulted in `r dim(conditions)[1]` conditions, with 500 replications per condition.

