## Performance metrics

For performance under correct specification, we evaluate methods with respect to their $\alpha$ error rate, and for performance under misspecification, with respect to their statistical power.
For hypothesis tests, we used $\alpha = .05$ as the significance level.
Based on analytical $p$-values and cutoff values for SEM indices recommended as rules-of-thumb to obtain good fit [RMSEA, SRMR\ \>\ \.05\; CFI, TLI\ \<\ \.97\; NFI\ \<\ \.95\; @schermelleh-engel2003], we computed rejection rates across replications in each condition.
Rejection rates based on bootstrapped $p$-values were computed for all methods to account for potential violations of distributional assumptions introduced by network estimation.
For the Mantel method, only bootstrapped $p$-values were used, as the analytical null distribution tests $H_0: r_g = 0$, whereas the modified method requires testing $H_0: r_g = 1$.

